ss and the border wall  is doing what  general Santa Hana could not do 150  years ago he couldn't push the border of  Mexico north of the Rio Grande River and  that is what the border wall is doing  the government's is also planning to  clear up to 200 yards on one side of the  levee to establish a control zone for  monitoring illegal entrance and it's  prepared to seize that land using  eminent domain you grew up in this  country you go to public school you may  learn about eminent domain but until  you're in the crosshairs of the  government you don't understand how it  really works they invoke it and they  take your land and they do what they  want but naty's not the only one  threatened by eminent domain rayon  saldua is descended from Spanish  settlers who in the 1700s were awarded  land grants in the valley totaling about  18,000 acres out of those 18,000 acres  now we probably have no more than 150  acres you know  so that's a lot of land to lose you know  and and here they come again with his  border wall on Sal duas first cousin  Fred Cavazos and Fred's sister Eloisa  Cavazos both live on a portion of the  family's property that will be cut into  the house where the Cavazos has lived  will end up on the US side of the wall  but much of their land and rental  properties fall on the Mexican side  making them so difficult to access  they'll likely become worthless building  the 200-yard control zone will mean  leveling the livestock pens and  additional rental properties belonging  to the Cavazos family this lab would  lose about ten acres and two barns and  three houses so when they asked me can  you help me cousin I say yes I I got  ties to this land too just like you do  this this is our grandmother's land and  Sal do is expecting the government to  make an offer to buy his cousin's land  but if the family declines the  government can move to take it any way  through eminent domain every time we  turn around somebody start trying to  take land from us and I said well this  for the good of the community what about  our own good it'll be who's compensating  us for for all this land that we're  losing this is enough we're gonna fight  it might be a losing battle but we still  little fight it but fighting could  backfire on the Cavazos family and the  valley landowners in 2008 when the Bush  administration was building an  immigration barrier in the neighboring  town of Brownsville the Department of  Homeland Security exploited loopholes in  the law meant to protect property owners  and in each case invoked a so called  declaration of taking which is a way to  expedite the eminent domain process  today landowners are still fighting for  what they consider just compensation for  the land they lost a decade ago there  are still 90 lawsuits open now because  their land was taken with no  compensation  90 lawsuits still open from almost ten  years ago and that's what will happen  again now further strengthening the  power of the executive branch to seize  land in the Rio Grande Valley is the  Real ID Act signed by George W Bush in  2005 as part of the administration's war  on terror it allows the government to  waive almost any law impeding the  construction of barriers and roads for  securing the border and there are 37  laws in the Real ID Act that the federal  government waives for border wall  construction they get to bulldoze tribal  lands grave sites historical landmarks  religious sites they do not have to  abide by the Clean Air Act or the Clean  Water Act the government has exempted  itself from all of the laws that govern  our country they've said they don't  apply to us for this purpose one  historic landmark that is no longer  protected thanks to the Real ID Act is  La Loma - chapel built in 1865 it sits  on a plot of land that will be  incorporated into the control zone the  city of Mission Texas is named for this  old mission on the banks of the Rio  Grande River it is a chapel that is in  use every day all day by members of our  community Nass is still given there this  is where we worship it as children this  is where my parents worship this is  where my grandparents worship this is  where my great-grandparents worship so  it's got a lot of significance for us  you know and we hate to lose this we  would hate to lose it  construction on the project hasn't  started yet but Customs and Border  Protection are a constant presence in  the area federal immigration law gives  the agency the authority to search and  patrol private property within 25 miles  of the border without obtaining a  warrant 24 hours a day  we are occupied by border patrol both  physically and virtually they drag tires  they have drones and helicopters they  have agents on foot on bicycle on dirt  bike on  on four-wheeler and SUV on boat on our  shores every kind of presence you can  imagine then they have planted  motion-activated sensors on our property  they have installed 24-hour video  surveillance our property probably the  Border Patrol here is about maybe three  or four times what it was even five  years ago so this border is pretty much  militarized the government however does  not have the authority at this point to  enter an alter private property in  preparation for the border wall but  treviño right says she encountered a  group of government contractors on the  butterfly centers property clearing  foliage that had been planted to host  butterflies and I was like what are you  guys doing here and they're like well  we're clear in this land I said my land  and the guide the young guy with the  orange vest and the safety goggles and  everything goes I think I should call my  supervisor and I said I think you should  do that too a few days later Manuel  Padilla the chief border patrol agent  for the Rio Grande Valley paid the  butterfly center a visit he showed up  unannounced with no appointment in plain  clothes and his attache and a uniformed  agent Padilla presented plans for the  border wall to treviño right and  according to her account  he said the contractors would be back  this time accompanied by a green  uniformed presence and so I asked him  about that later do I understand you  correctly that you are sending armed  federal agents onto private property to  protect your contractors naba decided to  take legal action suing the Department  of Homeland Security and Customs and  Border Protection part of our lawsuit is  that Patrol the definition of what is  patrolling  and do they really have the ability to  occupy our land our land according to  Border Patrol is theirs to do with as  they please after the lawsuit was filed  Border Patrol sent a form letter to  every property owner in the area asking  them for 18 months of irrevocable access  so the agency can survey the land to  prepare for construction many owners  including naba and the Cavazos family  refused to sign and I just got word this  week that the government has moved to  dismiss our lawsuit these issues will be  sorted out in court but regardless of  the outcome for the property owners of  the Rio Grande Valley the long-term  outlook is bleak they're literally  pulling the rug out from under our  community in terms of economic stability  jobs tax revenue and with nothing  to replace any of that  [Music]
 

